INTRODUCTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Item 2a — Scientific Rationale:
REPORTED: Contextualized within post-conflict Syrian health system resilience, local manufacturing capacity (>80% domestic demand), pharmacist density (1:613), WHO Workforce 2030, and FIP Development Goals (Goal 4 & Goal 20).

Item 2b — Specific Objectives & Hypotheses:
REPORTED: Explicitly states study objectives investigating attitudinal-implementation gaps, institutional readiness constraints, and sectoral practice participation.

METHODS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Item 3a — Study Design:
REPORTED: Cross-sectional observational quantitative survey design.

Item 3b — Setting & Dates:
REPORTED: Conducted in Syria between 10 March and 15 April 2026 via structured electronic data collection across faculties, syndicate channels, and manufacturing sites.

Item 3c — Eligibility Criteria:
REPORTED: Pharmacy degree holders or active undergraduate pharmacy students in Syria; exclusion of non-pharmacy backgrounds and incomplete submissions.

Item 4 — Variables & Operationalization:
REPORTED: Formative Composite Indices (PILS, IAI, IRS, IPI, Diagnostic Gap) defined with item counts, normalization (0–100 scale), and floor effect metrics.

Item 5 — Measurement & Data Sources:
REPORTED: Electronic survey platform with single-response technical enforcement. Formative measurement philosophy explicitly justified (Coltman et al., 2008).

Item 6a — Bias Control:
REPORTED: Single-response technical enforcement; convenience sampling acknowledged transparently as a study limitation.

Item 6b — Study Size:
REPORTED: Target 400 distributed invitations; 303 verified complete responses obtained (75.75% response rate).

Item 7 — Quantitative Variables:
REPORTED: Descriptive distributions reported with Mean, SD, Median, IQR, Min, Max, Skewness, and Zero-count/percentage.

Item 8 — Statistical Methods:
REPORTED: Spearman rank correlation, Kruskal-Wallis H-test, One-way ANOVA, Binary Logistic Regression, Binomial GLM.

RESULTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Item 9 — Participant Flow & Sample Demographics:
REPORTED: Final analytical sample (N = 303): 224 Undergraduate Students (73.93%), 54 Academic Researchers (17.82%), 25 Practicing Pharmacists (8.25%); 215 Females (70.96%), 88 Males (29.04%).

Item 10 — Descriptive Data:
REPORTED: PILS = 38.87 ± 15.55%, IAI = 71.30 ± 8.27%, IRS = 17.82 ± 20.35% (48.51% zeros), IPI = 10.45 ± 17.91% (71.95% zeros), Diagnostic Gap = 60.84 ± 19.93 points.

Item 11a/b — Associations & Multivariable Analysis:
REPORTED: Spearman correlation matrix (Table 3); Binary Logistic Regression and Binomial GLM models (Table 4) with Odds Ratios, 95% Confidence Intervals, and p-values.

